I'll move from high to medium, but not to low. The comment does state a behaviour, not only naming: "the code joins the two checks with &&, so it only passes if the user is both admin and owner". The code at the head:
  const isTeamAdminOrOwner =
    (await isTeamAdmin(user.id, booking.eventType?.teamId ?? 0)) &&
    (await isTeamOwner(user.id, booking.eventType?.teamId ?? 0));
  if (!isTeamAdminOrOwner && !isOrganizer && !isAttendee) throw FORBIDDEN
So a team admin who is not owner (and is not the organizer or an attendee) is refused when adding guests to a team booking. That is wrong access behaviour for an uncommon but real group of users — medium, not a pure rename.

I keep low. The comment opens with "It was not verified whether any caller passes Visualize instances" — it states no failing path, only a request to check. The function is typed to accept only GroupBy | BaseVisualize:
export function updateLocationWithAggregateFields(location, aggregateFields: Array<GroupBy | BaseVisualize> | null | undefined)
and its single caller (index.tsx:283) passes pageParams.aggregateFields, set through useSetExploreAggregateFields(aggregateFields: BaseAggregateField[]). Losing values only happens for a caller the comment never identifies; a "please verify" note is low.
